VALUES
  ($c$intellectual-property-agriculture-western-rajasthan$c$, $c$Intellectual Property in Agriculture in Western Rajasthan$c$, $c$D. Kumar, Saren K., R. S. Mehta, S. C. Meena$c$, $c$Intellectual Property (IP) refers to a category of property encompassing intangible creations of the human intellect. The most recognized forms of IP include patents, copyrights, trademarks, and trade secrets, although the scope and recognition of these rights vary across countries.$c$, $c$<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image1.webp" alt="Intellectual Property in Agriculture in Western Rajasthan" loading="lazy"></figure>
<p>Intellectual Property (IP) refers to a category of property encompassing intangible creations of the human intellect. The most recognized forms of IP include patents, copyrights, trademarks, and trade secrets, although the scope and recognition of these rights vary across countries.</p>
<p>The modern concept of intellectual property originated in England during the 17th and 18th centuries, and the term itself began to be used in the 19th century. However, it was not until the late 20th century that IP became an integral part of legal systems worldwide.</p>
<p>Supporters of intellectual property laws argue that their primary purpose is to promote creativity and innovation by granting creators and inventor’s exclusive rights to their intellectual outputs for a limited time. By doing so, these laws enable individuals and businesses to derive economic benefits from their creations, encouraging further innovation and technological progress.</p>
<p>Unlike tangible property such as land or goods, IP is intangible and indivisible, meaning it can be used by many people simultaneously without being depleted. This creates unique challenges in protecting IP, as creators cannot physically restrict access to their works in the same way landowners can protect their property. Therefore, the key objective of modern IP law is to balance protection and accessibility—ensuring creators are rewarded for innovation while allowing public access to knowledge and information.</p>
<p>According to Jean-Frédéric Morin, the global intellectual property regime is undergoing a paradigm shift. Until the early 2000s, the system was dominated by high protection standards modeled after European and U.S. laws, with limited regard for the economic or social conditions of developing nations. The new discourse, however, emphasizes policy flexibility and improved access to knowledge, particularly for developing countries.</p>
<p>The WIPO Development Agenda (2007) represents a major step in this direction. It includes 45 recommendations designed to align WIPO’s activities with the specific needs of developing nations, addressing issues such as access to medicines, information, seeds, source codes, and scientific research. Nonetheless, these progressive ideas have yet to result in substantial legal reforms at the international level.</p>
<p>The Trade-Related Aspects of Intellectual Property Rights (TRIPS) agreement, established in 1995 under the WTO, requires member nations to maintain minimum standards of IP protection. While it has significantly integrated intellectual property into the global trading system, it has also been controversial for its “one-size-fits-all” approach, which does not account for differences in national development levels. Despite ongoing debates, TRIPS remains the most comprehensive international agreement on IP rights.</p>
<h3>Rights under Intellectual Property</h3>
<p>Intellectual Property Rights (IPR) encompass patents, copyrights, industrial design rights, trademarks, plant variety rights, trade dress, geographical indications, and, in some jurisdictions, trade secrets. Specialized or sui generis forms include circuit design rights (mask work rights in the U.S.), supplementary protection certificates for pharmaceuticals, and database rights in European law.</p>
<p>The term “industrial property” is often used to refer to a subset of these rights—covering patents, trademarks, industrial designs, utility models, service marks, trade names, and geographical indications.</p>
<h3>Patents</h3>
<p>A patent is an exclusive right granted by a government to an inventor or their assignee, allowing them to exclude others from making, using, selling, offering for sale, or importing the invention for a limited period. In exchange, the inventor must publicly disclose details of the invention to advance scientific and technical knowledge.</p>
<p><strong>To qualify for a patent, an invention must meet three key criteria:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Novelty – It must be new and not previously known.</li>
<li>Non-obviousness – It must not be an obvious improvement to someone skilled in the field.</li>
<li>Industrial applicability – It must be capable of practical application in industry.</li>
</ol>
<p>Patents thus play a vital role in encouraging innovation while ensuring that valuable knowledge is shared with society.</p>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image1.webp$c$, 4, 'extension'),
  ($c$rural-knowledge-centers-jaisalmer$c$, $c$Advantages of Rural Knowledge Centers (RKC) in Jaisalmer$c$, $c$D. Kumar, Saren K., R. S. Mehta, S. C. Meena$c$, $c$Rural Knowledge Centers (RKC) aim to bridge the information gap between rural and urban areas by providing access to modern communication tools and relevant local information. These centers empower rural families, especially youth and women, by improving access to knowledge, training, and technology.$c$, $c$<p>Rural Knowledge Centers (RKC) aim to bridge the information gap between rural and urban areas by providing access to modern communication tools and relevant local information. These centers empower rural families, especially youth and women, by improving access to knowledge, training, and technology.</p>
<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image2.webp" alt="Advantages of Rural Knowledge Centers (RKC) in Jaisalmer" loading="lazy"></figure>
<h3>Key Activities</h3>
<ul>
<li>Establish village information centers providing access to modern information, internet connectivity, and communication technology.</li>
<li>Train educated rural youth, especially women, to manage and operate these information centers.</li>
<li>Develop systems to create locally relevant information from general data and train youth in maintaining them.</li>
<li>Maintain and update information on government schemes and entitlements for rural families using modern and traditional communication channels.</li>
<li>Provide timely updates and information on government relief measures during disasters.</li>
<li>Conduct surveys, participatory rural appraisals, and impact assessments to evaluate program effectiveness.</li>
<li>Build models for efficient information exchange using ICT (Information and Communication Technology).</li>
<li>Add value to information by using local languages to make it more accessible.</li>
<li>Promote research on forming sustainable partnerships between government, private sector, NGOs, and rural communities to strengthen ICT-based models.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Benefits to Farmers</h3>
<ul>
<li>Easy access to agricultural resources and schemes from the administration.</li>
<li>Direct access to agricultural databases, improving communication between farmers and agricultural departments.</li>
<li>Availability of real-time data such as soil conditions, land patterns, and input usage for better decision-making.</li>
<li>Information on government schemes and relief measures during natural calamities or crop diseases.</li>
<li>Enhanced decision-making through ground-level data collection and sharing with higher officials.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Establishment of Rural Knowledge Centers</h3>
<p><strong>Actions Taken So Far:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Basic infrastructure such as furniture, TVs, DVDs, public address systems, display boards, and stationery has been procured and distributed to all RKCs.</li>
<li>Educational materials like books, booklets, leaflets, brochures, charts, and posters have been provided.</li>
<li>Computer systems are in the final stage of procurement and expected to arrive shortly.</li>
<li>Telephone connections are being established; internet connectivity (broadband or dial-up) will follow immediately.</li>
<li>A central hub for the RKCs will be set up at the Directorate of Agriculture, Haddo, Port Blair.</li>
<li>Computer-literate personnel will soon be appointed to manage electronic systems in each RKC for smooth operation.</li>
</ul>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image2.webp$c$, 2, 'ict-in-agriculture'),
  ($c$village-knowledge-centers-arid-zone$c$, $c$Role of Village Knowledge Centers (VKC) in the Development of Villages in the Arid Zone$c$, $c$D. Kumar, Saren K., R. S. Mehta, S. C. Meena$c$, $c$Village Knowledge Centers (VKC) serve as information hubs that provide farmers instant access to the latest knowledge in agriculture — from crop production to marketing. Each center is managed by a “VKC In-charge” responsible for day-to-day operations.$c$, $c$<p>Village Knowledge Centers (VKC) serve as information hubs that provide farmers instant access to the latest knowledge in agriculture — from crop production to marketing. Each center is managed by a “VKC In-charge” responsible for day-to-day operations.</p>
<h3>Importance</h3>
<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image3.webp" alt="Role of Village Knowledge Centers (VKC) in the Development of Villages in the Arid Zone" loading="lazy"></figure>
<p>India’s vast and diverse population presents unique development challenges. For economic growth to be truly sustainable, it must include and empower rural communities — home to most of the population. VKCs contribute to this inclusive growth by improving rural livelihoods, enabling knowledge sharing, and building stronger, more self-reliant villages.This initiative, supported by the Bank, is part of a broader mission to promote sustainable rural development.</p>
<h3>Mission</h3>
<p>To harness the power of information and communication technologies for the knowledge, skill, economic, and social empowerment of rural families.</p>
<h3>Objectives</h3>
<ul>
<li>Encourage reading habits among children through village libraries established under VKCs.</li>
<li>Introduce children to computer literacy and basic digital skills.</li>
<li>Connect villagers to the outside world through newspapers, magazines, computers, radios, and televisions available at VKCs.</li>
</ul>
<p>This project follows the model developed by the M.S. Swaminathan Research Foundation. Two village libraries under the VKC initiative are successfully running in Raila and Dander villages (Pilani chapter).Additionally, Sankalp, an organization from a U.S. university, is funding two more libraries and upgrading one into a full-fledged VKC. New VKCs are planned near Pilani, Hyderabad, and Warangal.</p>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image3.webp$c$, 2, 'ict-in-agriculture'),
  ($c$precision-farming-western-rajasthan$c$, $c$Precision Farming in Western Rajasthan$c$, $c$D. Kumar, Saren K., R. S. Mehta, S. C. Meena$c$, $c$Precision farming (also called precision agriculture) is a modern farm management method that observes, measures, and responds to variations within fields to optimize crop production. It relies on advanced tools such as satellite imagery, GPS, geospatial tools, and information technology. Using these technologies, farmers can identify their exact position in the field and apply inputs—like water,…$c$, $c$<p>Precision farming (also called precision agriculture) is a modern farm management method that observes, measures, and responds to variations within fields to optimize crop production. It relies on advanced tools such as satellite imagery, GPS, geospatial tools, and information technology. Using these technologies, farmers can identify their exact position in the field and apply inputs—like water, fertilizers, and pesticides—only where needed.</p>
<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image4.webp" alt="Precision Farming in Western Rajasthan" loading="lazy"></figure>
<h3>Goals of Precision Agriculture</h3>
<p>Precision farming aims to optimize crop production at the field level by focusing on:</p>
<ul>
<li>Crop Science: Matching farm practices to actual crop requirements (e.g., applying fertilizers only where needed).</li>
<li>Environmental Protection: Reducing environmental risks such as nutrient leaching and pollution.</li>
<li>Economics: Improving efficiency and reducing costs by managing inputs more precisely.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Additionally, it helps farmers to:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Maintain detailed farm records.</li>
<li>Improve farm decision-making and traceability.</li>
<li>Strengthen relationships with landlords and buyers.</li>
<li>Enhance product quality and marketability (e.g., improving protein content in wheat).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Stages and Tools of Precision Farming</h3>
<h3>1. Data Collection (Relocation)</h3>
<p><strong>Farmers collect and organize field data by:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>GPS-based mapping: Using a GPS receiver while driving through the field.</li>
<li>Aerial or satellite imagery: Creating detailed base maps for field planning.</li>
</ul>
<p>This helps overlay data about soil type, nutrient levels, and previous crops for accurate field analysis.</p>
<h3>2. Characterizing Variability</h3>
<p><strong>Variations within and between fields can occur due to:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Climate factors: Rain, drought, hail, etc.</li>
<li>Soil conditions: Texture, depth, and nitrogen levels.</li>
<li>Farming practices: Tillage, pest management, or irrigation methods.</li>
</ul>
<p>Farmers use soil sensors, weather stations, remote sensing, and satellite imagery to monitor these differences.Soil resistivity tests and other analyses allow precise mapping of soil and crop conditions.</p>
<h3>3. Decision-Making</h3>
<p><strong>Farmers use two main strategies to adjust inputs:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Predictive Approach: Based on static data like soil properties and field history before planting.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Control Approach: Based on real-time monitoring during the crop cycle through:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Sampling: Measuring biomass, leaf color, and fruit weight.</li>
<li>Remote sensing: Using sensors to measure temperature, humidity, or soil moisture.</li>
<li>In-field sensors: Mounted on tractors to measure plant health.</li>
<li>Satellite or aerial imaging: Producing multispectral images to assess crop growth.</li>
</ul>
<p>Farmers use decision-support systems and crop models to interpret this data, but final decisions depend on both economic returns and environmental considerations.</p>
<h3>4. Implementing Variable Practices</h3>
<p>Modern technologies make site-specific crop management easier. Equipment using Variable Rate Technology (VRT) can automatically adjust seed rate, fertilizer, and pesticide applications.</p>
<p><strong>Common tools include:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>GPS systems: Determine exact position in the field.</li>
<li>GIS software: Integrates and analyzes all spatial data.</li>
<li>Variable-rate machinery: Controls input application rates accurately.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Global Adoption</h3>
<p>Precision agriculture began in the United States in the 1980s, with researchers experimenting with variable lime applications and grid sampling. By the 1990s, GPS and yield sensors became integral to farm operations.</p>
<p>Countries like the U.S., Canada, Australia, the U.K., and France were early adopters. In France, precision farming became established around 1997–1998, supported by the development of GPS-guided equipment and fertilizer mapping.Today, GPS use is widespread, though variable-rate systems are still used by less than 10% of farmers in some countries.</p>
<h3><strong>Economic and Environmental Impac</strong>t</h3>
<p><strong>Precision farming provides multiple benefits:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Economic: Reduces input costs (fertilizers, pesticides) while maintaining or increasing yields.</li>
<li>Environmental: Minimizes pollution and resource waste by applying inputs only where and when needed.</li>
<li>Sustainability: Protects soil and water resources, ensuring long-term agricultural productivity.</li>
</ul>
<p>By combining technology, science, and sustainable practices, precision farming allows farmers to produce more with fewer resources, supporting both profitability and environmental conservation.</p>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image4.webp$c$, 3, 'agri-tech'),
  ($c$ict-tools-agriculture-development-thar-desert$c$, $c$Importance of ICT for Agriculture Development in the Thar Desert$c$, $c$D. Kumar, R. S. Mehta, Shiren K., S. C. Meena$c$, $c$Information and Communication Technology (ICT) plays a vital role in improving agricultural productivity, efficiency, and sustainability. It helps farmers, researchers, and policymakers make informed decisions through timely access to information, modern tools, and communication networks. Key ICT Tools in Agriculture$c$, $c$<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image5.webp" alt="Importance of ICT for Agriculture Development in the Thar Desert" loading="lazy"></figure>
<p>Information and Communication Technology (ICT) plays a vital role in improving agricultural productivity, efficiency, and sustainability. It helps farmers, researchers, and policymakers make informed decisions through timely access to information, modern tools, and communication networks. Key ICT Tools in Agriculture</p>
<h3>1. Geographic Information System (GIS)</h3>
<p>A Geographic Information System (GIS) is a computer-based tool that captures, stores, analyzes, and displays geographic data. It helps in mapping soil types, land use, crop patterns, and water resources.Use in agriculture: GIS supports precision farming, resource management, and land-use planning.</p>
<h3>2. Remote Sensing</h3>
<p>Remote sensing involves collecting information about the Earth’s surface without direct contact, using satellites or sensors.Applications:</p>
<ul>
<li>Crop monitoring and yield estimation</li>
<li>Drought and flood assessment</li>
<li>Soil and water management</li>
<li>Weather forecasting</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. E-Governance</h3>
<p>E-Governance refers to the use of information technology to deliver government services efficiently. It connects:</p>
<ul>
<li>G2C (Government to Citizen)</li>
<li>G2B (Government to Business)</li>
<li>G2G (Government to Government)</li>
<li>G2E (Government to Employees)</li>
</ul>
<p>Benefits: Enhances transparency, reduces delays, and ensures better delivery of agricultural schemes and subsidies.</p>
<h3>4. Cyber Extension</h3>
<p>Cyber Extension uses online learning platforms and digital communication to transfer agricultural knowledge and training to farmers and extension workers.Benefits:</p>
<ul>
<li>Provides online training and expert advice</li>
<li>Supports distance learning for rural youth</li>
<li>Enhances communication between farmers and researchers</li>
</ul>
<h3>Role of ICT in Agricultural Development</h3>
<ol>
<li>Promotes economic development through better planning and access to market information.</li>
<li>Strengthens community development and local participation.</li>
<li>Supports research and education through knowledge sharing.</li>
<li>Encourages small and medium enterprise development in rural areas.</li>
<li>Expands new media networks for agricultural communication.</li>
<li>Provides decision support for planning and monitoring development programs.</li>
<li>Improves service delivery and ensures transparency in governance.</li>
<li>Empowers citizens by providing access to information and knowledge.</li>
<li>Facilitates training and skill development for agricultural workers.</li>
<li>Enhances market linkages and service delivery to small and medium farmers.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Conclusion</h3>
<p>ICT is transforming agriculture by making it more efficient, knowledge-driven, and responsive. It bridges the information gap, strengthens extension systems, and supports sustainable rural development.</p>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image5.webp$c$, 2, 'ict-in-agriculture'),
  ($c$bridging-digital-divide-arid-western-rajasthan$c$, $c$Bridging the Digital Divide in Arid Western Rajasthan$c$, $c$D. Kumar, R. S. Mehta, Shiren K., S. C. Meena$c$, $c$The digital divide refers to the gap between people who have access to modern information and communication technology (ICT) and those who do not.It can be seen in two main forms: Language Divide: Between English and non-English speaking communities, where useful information is often available only in English.$c$, $c$<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image6.webp" alt="Bridging the Digital Divide in Arid Western Rajasthan" loading="lazy"></figure>
<p>The digital divide refers to the gap between people who have access to modern information and communication technology (ICT) and those who do not.It can be seen in two main forms: Language Divide: Between English and non-English speaking communities, where useful information is often available only in English.</p>
<ul>
<li>North–South Divide: Between developed and developing countries in terms of ICT access and usage.</li>
</ul>
<h3>National-Level Initiatives in ICT for Agricultural Development</h3>
<p>Several initiatives have been launched in India to use ICT for improving agricultural communication and services:</p>
<ul>
<li>Gyandoot Project</li>
<li>Warna Wired Village Project</li>
<li>Information Village Project (MSSRF)</li>
<li>I-Kisan Project by Nagarjuna Group of Companies (Andhra Pradesh)</li>
<li>AMUL Dairy Cooperative – Milk collection centers (Gujarat)</li>
<li>Tarahaat.com by Development Alternatives (Uttar Pradesh and Punjab)</li>
<li>Land Record Computerization (Bhoomi Project)</li>
<li>SRISTI – Knowledge Network for Grassroots Innovations (Gujarat)</li>
<li>Mahiti Samuha (Karnataka)</li>
<li>Centre for Alternative Agriculture Media (CAAM)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Networking System of Information</h3>
<p>Networking connects computers to share information and resources, either locally (LAN) or globally (WAN).Effective networking improves productivity and efficiency, reduces costs, and helps gain competitive advantages by making optimal use of business and agricultural resources.</p>
<h3>Teleconferencing</h3>
<p>Modern digital communication systems allow teleconferencing, enabling people to hold meetings from different locations without traveling. Conference rooms equipped with cameras and digital tools make it possible to share visuals and discussions in real time.</p>
<p><strong>Advantages:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Reduces time and cost of daily communication.</li>
<li>Promotes international collaboration and integration.</li>
<li>Helps in sharing and exchanging the latest agricultural technologies across countries.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Types of Teleconferencing:a) Audio Conferencingb) Video Conferencing</h3>
<h3>Problems of ICT</h3>
<ol>
<li>Poor infrastructure</li>
<li>Illiteracy</li>
<li>Poverty</li>
<li>Policy barriers</li>
<li>Low public participation</li>
<li>Weak implementation</li>
<li>Uneven technology adoption</li>
<li>Digital divide</li>
</ol>
<h3>Prospects of ICT</h3>
<ol>
<li>Better marketing information</li>
<li>Use of remote sensing</li>
<li>Decision Support Systems (DSS)</li>
<li>Knowledge sharing and realization</li>
<li>Strengthening of extension organizations</li>
<li>Integration with agricultural extension</li>
<li>Addressing globalization challenges</li>
<li>Online services – information, education, training, monitoring, evaluation</li>
<li>E-commerce opportunities</li>
<li>Research–Extension–Farmer (R–E–F) interaction</li>
<li>Question–answer services for farmers</li>
<li>E-governance</li>
<li>Real-time information updates</li>
<li>Database creation</li>
<li>Prediction tools for diseases, pests, and weather</li>
<li>Land record management and facilitation</li>
</ol>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image6.webp$c$, 2, 'ict-in-agriculture'),
  ($c$rural-technology-parks-arid-agriculture-western-rajasthan$c$, $c$Importance of Technology Parks / Rural Tech Parks in Arid Agriculture of Western Rajasthan$c$, $c$D. Kumar, R. S. Mehta, Shiren K., S. C. Meena$c$, $c$To promote agricultural and food processing industries in clusters located in areas with high production of process able agricultural and horticultural products.$c$, $c$<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image7.webp" alt="Importance of Technology Parks / Rural Tech Parks in Arid Agriculture of Western Rajasthan" loading="lazy"></figure>
<p>To promote agricultural and food processing industries in clusters located in areas with high production of process able agricultural and horticultural products.</p>
<h3>Specific Objectives</h3>
<ol>
<li>Facilitate quick dissemination of agricultural information to farmers, students, and the general public.</li>
<li>Serve as a demonstration and learning center showcasing the latest recommendations and technologies from both public and private institutions.</li>
<li>Provide a platform to study traditional heritage agricultural systems as well as future high-tech agricultural models.</li>
<li>Establish a live crop museum for educational and training purposes.</li>
<li>Create an information network connecting farmers, agricultural officers, government departments, and research institutes.</li>
<li>Develop aesthetic landscaping to enhance the park’s visual appeal.</li>
<li>Promote eco-tourism and agro-tourism activities.</li>
<li>Ensure self-sustainability through internal fund generation.</li>
<li>Function as a “one-stop shop” for the sale of plants, seeds, media materials, and agricultural products from government and private sectors.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Main Elements of Agro / Tech Parks</h3>
<ol>
<li>Venue: Should be located near clusters of agricultural institutions.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Main Sections:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Jeera section</li>
<li>Vegetable garden</li>
<li>Root and tuber crops area</li>
<li>Fruit orchard</li>
<li>Dryland crops section</li>
<li>Exhibition Stalls: Display of major crops and technologies.</li>
<li>Nursery: For demonstration and sales of plants.</li>
<li>Mushroom Production Unit: For demonstration and training.</li>
<li>Food Technology Demonstration Unit.</li>
<li>Floriculture Area: Demonstration and sales zone for private sector involvement.</li>
<li>Traditional Agriculture Zone: Showcasing indigenous agricultural systems of India.</li>
<li>High-Tech Agriculture Zone: Demonstration of modern, advanced farming technologies.</li>
<li>Student Learning Area: Discovery-based learning zone for students.</li>
<li>Meteorological Station.</li>
<li>Animal Production and Health Section.</li>
<li>Export-Oriented Crop Section.</li>
<li>Sales Centre: For agri-products, seeds, and processed goods.</li>
<li>Information Centre: Providing agricultural updates and guidance.</li>
<li>Food Courts: Offering local and value-added food products.</li>
<li>Resting Areas: For visitors and tourists.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Policy Initiative</h3>
<p>The Government of India has emphasized the development of world-class infrastructure for agro and food processing industries through the establishment of Agro Food Technology Parks.</p>
<ul>
<li>Each park receives financial assistance of ₹4 crore from the Government of India.</li>
<li>These parks are designed to provide information, training, research, and development (R&amp;D) support to farmers and entrepreneurs.</li>
<li>Agricultural and horticultural farms located within a radius of 80–100 km are linked to the nearest Agro Food Technology Park for better connectivity and access.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Common Facilities and Infrastructure</h3>
<ul>
<li>Research and Development (R&amp;D) Centres</li>
<li>Warehousing Facilities</li>
<li>Cold Storage Units</li>
<li>Quality Assurance Laboratories</li>
<li>Common Effluent Treatment Plants</li>
</ul>
