   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image7.webp$c$, 3, 'agri-tech'),
  ($c$atic-cazri-jodhpur-farmers-development$c$, $c$Agriculture Technology Information Centre (ATIC) of CAZRI Jodhpur: Role in Farmers' Development$c$, $c$D. Kumar, R. S. Mehta, Shiren K., S. C. Meena$c$, $c$The Agriculture Technology Information Centre (ATIC) acts as “single-window” support system connecting research institutions with farmers, entrepreneurs, and other users. It helps in decision-making and problem-solving by providing easy access to agricultural technologies, services, and information.$c$, $c$<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image8.webp" alt="Agriculture Technology Information Centre (ATIC) of CAZRI Jodhpur: Role in Farmers' Development" loading="lazy"></figure>
<p>The Agriculture Technology Information Centre (ATIC) acts as “single-window” support system connecting research institutions with farmers, entrepreneurs, and other users. It helps in decision-making and problem-solving by providing easy access to agricultural technologies, services, and information.</p>
<h3>Purpose of Establishing ATIC</h3>
<ol>
<li>To provide diagnostic services for soil, water, plant, and livestock health.</li>
<li>To supply research products such as improved seeds and planting materials.</li>
<li>To disseminate information through printed and audio-visual materials.</li>
<li>To help research institutions and SAUs generate income by selling their technologies and products.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Key Criteria for Effective ATICs</h3>
<ol>
<li>Availability of new and relevant technologies.</li>
<li>Timely response to farmers’ needs.</li>
<li>Sustainability of ATIC within the institutional framework.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Target Users / Beneficiaries</h3>
<ul>
<li>Farmers</li>
<li>Farmer-entrepreneurs</li>
<li>Extension and development workers</li>
<li>NGOs</li>
<li>Private sector organizations</li>
</ul>
<h3>Objectives(ATIC)jodhpur pic</h3>
<ol>
<li>To serve as a single window for technology delivery and services.</li>
<li>To improve farmers’ access to institutional resources and information.</li>
<li>To establish a feedback mechanism from users to research institutions.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Services Provided by ATIC</h3>
<ol>
<li>Soil and water testing facilities</li>
<li>Plant health clinic and diagnostic services</li>
<li>Supply of Rhizobium culture and biofertilizers</li>
<li>Integrated Pest Management (IPM) and biopesticides</li>
<li>Supply of quality seed, planting materials, and small implements</li>
<li>Fertilizer quality testing</li>
<li>Tissue culture plants and nursery materials</li>
<li>Farm literature – leaflets, pamphlets, journals</li>
<li>Audio-visual learning materials (videos, cassettes, shows)</li>
<li>Display and exhibition through Technology Parks</li>
<li>Processed agricultural products (e.g., cereals, milk, meat)</li>
<li>Cafeteria for visitors</li>
<li>Veterinary and animal health services</li>
<li>Distribution of poultry strains and livestock breeds</li>
</ol>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image8.webp$c$, 2, 'extension'),
  ($c$e-governance-agriculture-western-rajasthan$c$, $c$Importance of E-Governance in Agriculture for Increasing Production in Western Rajasthan$c$, $c$D. Kumar, R. S. Mehta, Shiren K., S. C. Meena$c$, $c$E-Governance, as defined by Bakus (2001), is the use of electronic means to improve:$c$, $c$<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image9.webp" alt="Importance of E-Governance in Agriculture for Increasing Production in Western Rajasthan" loading="lazy"></figure>
<p>E-Governance, as defined by Bakus (2001), is the use of electronic means to improve:</p>
<ul>
<li>Interaction between government and citizens, and between government and businesses.</li>
<li>Internal government operations to simplify and enhance democratic, administrative, and business processes.</li>
</ul>
<p>It can also be described as transforming the relationship between governments and citizens using information technology.</p>
<h3>Benefits of E-Governance</h3>
<p><strong>Direct Benefits:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Faster, easier, and more convenient services close to home.</li>
<li>Reduced processing times and shorter waiting periods.</li>
<li>Fewer visits to government offices.</li>
<li>Improved interaction with government officials; less harassment.</li>
<li>Reduced transport costs and less wage loss for daily wage earners.</li>
<li>Higher quality services with accurate, legible, and error-free documentation.</li>
<li>Reduced corruption and increased transparency.</li>
<li>Better access to government offices.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Indirect/Derived Benefits:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Efficient complaint handling and tracking.</li>
<li>Transparency in government data, decisions, procedures, and performance.</li>
<li>Automation reduces discretion in decision-making.</li>
<li>Simplifies rules and processes; traceable and accountable actions.</li>
<li>Easier documentation and follow-up for citizens.</li>
<li>Tracks procedural delays and human errors.</li>
<li>Encourages outsourcing through modular tasks.</li>
<li>Promotes competition among delivery channels and departments.</li>
<li>Standardized documentation improves supervision and comparison.</li>
<li>Centralized data allows better auditing, analysis, and integration.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Critical Success Factors</h3>
<ol>
<li>Political will</li>
<li>National vision and e-strategy</li>
<li>Full support of Chief Technology Officer (CTO)</li>
<li>Effective project management</li>
<li>Accessibility</li>
<li>Client education</li>
<li>Client participation</li>
<li>Cultural readiness</li>
<li>Private sector collaboration</li>
<li>Appropriate technology</li>
<li>Skilled human resources</li>
<li>Legal framework</li>
<li>Adequate financing</li>
</ol>
<h3>Components of E-Governance</h3>
<ul>
<li>Political</li>
<li>Technological</li>
<li>Social</li>
<li>Cultural</li>
<li>Psychological</li>
<li>Service</li>
</ul>
<p>Interaction: All components work together to form an E-Governance ecosystem.</p>
<h3>Stages of E-Governance</h3>
<p><strong>Progress of government towards full E-Governance:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Presence – Government is online with basic information.</li>
<li>Interaction – Citizens can interact and communicate digitally.</li>
<li>Transaction – Citizens can complete transactions online.</li>
<li>Transformation – Complete integration and restructuring of processes.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Layne and Lee (2001) Framework:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Cataloguing – Online availability of information.</li>
<li>Transaction – Digital services and online transactions.</li>
<li>Vertical Integration – Linking different levels of government.</li>
<li>Horizontal Integration – Coordination across departments and agencies.</li>
</ol>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image9.webp$c$, 2, 'ict-in-agriculture'),
  ($c$cyber-extension-value-addition-jaisalmer$c$, $c$Role of Cyber Extension in Value Addition in Jaisalmer District of Western Rajasthan$c$, $c$D. Kumar, R. S. Mehta, A. Patidar, S. C. Meena$c$, $c$Cyber extension refers to delivering agricultural information and services through online networks. It uses computers and communication technologies to share knowledge in the form of text, graphics, audio, and video, either passively or interactively, helping farmers access the latest agricultural technologies.$c$, $c$<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image10.webp" alt="Role of Cyber Extension in Value Addition in Jaisalmer District of Western Rajasthan" loading="lazy"></figure>
<p>Cyber extension refers to delivering agricultural information and services through online networks. It uses computers and communication technologies to share knowledge in the form of text, graphics, audio, and video, either passively or interactively, helping farmers access the latest agricultural technologies.</p>
<p><strong>Cyber Extension Tools:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>E-mail</li>
<li>Interactive expert systems for crops, pests, and diseases</li>
<li>Internet-based agricultural information portals</li>
<li>Video conferencing</li>
<li>Call centers and SATCOM networks</li>
<li>Discussion forums and news groups</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Types of ICT Initiatives in Agriculture:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Central Government: NATP ITD component, ICAR extension programs, MIC initiative in Maharashtra.</li>
<li>State Government: Maharashtra info kiosks, Kerala Akshaya project for village panchayats.</li>
<li>NGOs: MSSRF’s Information Village Project, rural connectivity providers like Drishtee and N-Logue.</li>
<li>Private/Cooperative Sector: ITC, Mahindra, EDD – mainly transaction-based initiatives.</li>
<li>Information Services: Ikisan, Community India, Agriwatch, India Agro Net.</li>
<li>Multiple Rural Services: WARNA, Wyandot, Taranaat – offering agricultural extension.</li>
<li>Knowledge Networks/Banks: Honey Bee Network, Harit Gyan, Indian Society of AB professionals.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Current Benefits of Cyber Extension:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Acts as a complementary mechanism to traditional agricultural extension.</li>
<li>Integrated into undergraduate and postgraduate agricultural curricula in Indian State Agriculture Universities (e.g., IGNOU).</li>
<li>Supported by the Government of India for agricultural extension reform.</li>
<li>Initiatives like Kisan Call Center (KCC) enhance communication between farmers and experts.</li>
<li>Improves competitiveness and market access for Indian agricultural products through timely information dissemination.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Cyber Extension Process:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Develop modern ICT infrastructure connecting key stakeholders.</li>
<li>Promote ICT awareness across all development departments.</li>
<li>Create information packaging mechanisms at key agencies.</li>
<li>Network with state/district-level e-governance initiatives.</li>
<li>Establish model cells in each state to monitor progress.</li>
<li>Identify a nodal agency for cyber extension in India.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Services/Benefits Provided by Cyber Extension:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Facilitates interaction between farmers, extension workers, and rural communities.</li>
<li>Provides up-to-date news, information, and Q&amp;A services.</li>
<li>Maintains statistical databases.</li>
<li>Shares details of government schemes, their implementation, and poverty alleviation programs.</li>
<li>Hosts websites of agricultural institutions.</li>
<li>Launches online rural development and extension journals.</li>
<li>Offers internet access to district and block-level agricultural officers.</li>
<li>Sets up cyber cafes for rural communities and extension workers.</li>
<li>Provides maps showing population, crops, and other features.</li>
<li>Offers video demonstrations of agricultural procedures.</li>
<li>Enables user feedback on public sector schemes.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Top of Form</h3>
<h3>Bottom of Form</h3>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image10.webp$c$, 2, 'ict-in-agriculture'),
  ($c$dynamics-of-e-learning$c$, $c$Dynamics of E-Learning in Hyper-Arid Jaisalmer$c$, $c$D. Kumar, R. S. Mehta, A. Patidar, S. C. Meena$c$, $c$Electronic learning is a type of technology supported education/learning (TSL) where the medium of instruction is through computer technology, particularly involving digital technologies. E-learning has been defined as pedagogy empowered by digital technology. In some instances no face-to-face interaction takes place.E-learning services have evolved since computers were first used in education…$c$, $c$<p>Electronic learning is a type of technology supported education/learning (TSL) where the medium of instruction is through computer technology, particularly involving digital technologies. E-learning has been defined as pedagogy empowered by digital technology. In some instances no face-to-face interaction takes place.E-learning services have evolved since computers were first used in education there is a trend to move toward included learning services, where computer based activities are integrated with practical or classroom situation.</p>
<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image10.webp" alt="Dynamics of E-Learning in Hyper-Arid Jaisalmer" loading="lazy"></figure>
<h3>Pedagogical approaches or perspectives:-</h3>
<p>It is possible to use various pedagogical approaches for e-learning which includes instructional design. The traditional pedagogy of instruction which is curriculum focused and is developed by a centralized educating group or a single teacher.</p>
<ol>
<li>Social Constructivist</li>
<li>Cognitive perspective</li>
<li>Emotional perspective</li>
<li>Behavioral perspective</li>
<li>Contextual perspective</li>
</ol>
<h3>Benefits of e-learning versus traditional classroom setting:-</h3>
<ol>
<li>Reducing environmental impact</li>
<li>Quality education, made affordable</li>
<li>Convenience and flexibility to learners.</li>
</ol>
<p>Communication technologies used in e-learning: - Communication technologies are generally categorized as synchronized or a synchronous. A synchronous activities use technology such as blogs, wikis and discussion bonds.</p>
<p>Synchronous: - Involve the exchange of ideas and information with one or more participants during the same period of time. A face to face discussion is an example of synchronous communication.</p>
<p>lesions are generally designed to guide students perform in specific tasks. Information based learning content communicates info to the students. Examples include content that distributes the history or facts related to a service, company or product. Information based content there is no specific skill to be learned. In performance based content to lesions build off a procedural skill in which the student is expected to increase proficiency.</p>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image10.webp$c$, 2, 'ict-in-agriculture'),
  ($c$mis-for-managing-agriculture-in-thar-desert$c$, $c$Importance of Management Information System (MIS) for Managing Agriculture in the Thar Desert$c$, $c$D. Kumar, R. S. Mehta, Shiran K., S. C. Meena$c$, $c$A management information system (MIS) is an information system used for decision-making, and for the coordination, control, analysis, and visualization of information in an organization. The study of the management information systems involves people, processes and technology in an organizational context. In other words, it serves, as the functions of controlling, planning, decision making in the…$c$, $c$<p>A management information system (MIS) is an information system used for decision-making, and for the coordination, control, analysis, and visualization of information in an organization. The study of the management information systems involves people, processes and technology in an organizational context. In other words, it serves, as the functions of controlling, planning, decision making in the management level setting.</p>
<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image11.webp" alt="Importance of Management Information System (MIS) for Managing Agriculture in the Thar Desert" loading="lazy"></figure>
<h3>Evolution 1990</h3>
<h3>1. Electronic data processing</h3>
<h3>2. Computer based information system</h3>
<h3>3. Decision support system</h3>
<h3>4. Artificial intelligence</h3>
<h3>Uses of MIS on different levels of management:-</h3>
<h3>1. Top management</h3>
<h3>2. Middle Management</h3>
<h3>3. Lower Management</h3>
<h3>Design</h3>
<h3>1. Assessing: - Information needs for planning monitoring</h3>
<h3>2. Deciding: - Deciding the levels of info</h3>
<p>3. Ensuring: - System flexibility and adaptability.</p>
<h3>Guidelines for effective design of MIS:-</h3>
<h3>1. User participation</h3>
<h3>2. Cost evaluation</h3>
<h3>3. Alternative for in-house software development</h3>
<h3>4. Relevance and selectively of information</h3>
<h3>5. Pretesting of the system</h3>
<h3>6. Training the operators and users carefully</h3>
<h3>Classification of MIS:-</h3>
<p>Mason and Swanson 1981 describe four categories of management information system:-</p>
<h3>1. Database information system</h3>
<h3>2. Predictive</h3>
<h3>3. Decision Making</h3>
<h3>4. Decision Making</h3>
<h3>Roles of MIS:-</h3>
<p>1. To plan the most effective allocation of resources.</p>
<p>2. To choose between alterative course of action.</p>
<p>3. To control day to day operations.</p>$c$,
