</ul>
<p><strong>Goals:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Strengthen agriculture and irrigation sectors</li>
<li>Increase agricultural production</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Facilities Provided:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>IT services</li>
<li>Training programs</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Information Available:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Project-related activities</li>
<li>Printed materials</li>
<li>Irrigation networks</li>
<li>Rules, regulations, and agreements</li>
<li>Internet access</li>
<li>Water availability data</li>
<li>Laser land leveling information</li>
<li>Dedicated websites</li>
</ul>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image12.webp$c$, 1, 'ict-in-agriculture'),
  ($c$mis-increasing-production-arid-western-rajasthan$c$, $c$Importance of Management Information System (MIS) in Increasing Production in the Arid Region of Western Rajasthan$c$, $c$D. Kumar, R. S. Mehta, Shiran K., S. C. Meena$c$, $c$A Management Information System (MIS) is used for decision-making, planning, monitoring, and control in organizations. It integrates people, processes, and technology to support management functions.$c$, $c$<p>A Management Information System (MIS) is used for decision-making, planning, monitoring, and control in organizations. It integrates people, processes, and technology to support management functions.</p>
<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image14.webp" alt="Importance of Management Information System (MIS) in Increasing Production in the Arid Region of Western Rajasthan" loading="lazy"></figure>
<p><strong>Evolution of MIS (Since 1990):</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Electronic Data Processing</li>
<li>Computer-Based Information Systems</li>
<li>Decision Support Systems</li>
<li>Artificial Intelligence</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Uses across Management Levels:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Top Management</li>
<li>Middle Management</li>
<li>Lower Management</li>
</ul>
<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image15.webp" alt="" loading="lazy"></figure>
<p><strong>Design Steps:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Assess: Identify information needs for planning and monitoring</li>
<li>Decide: Determine levels of information required</li>
<li>Ensure: Maintain system flexibility and adaptability</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Guidelines for Effective MIS Design:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Involve users in the design process</li>
<li>Evaluate costs</li>
<li>Consider in-house vs. external software development</li>
<li>Ensure relevance and selective information</li>
<li>Pretest the system</li>
<li>Train operators and users</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Classification (Mason &amp; Swanson, 1981):</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Database Information System</li>
<li>Predictive System</li>
<li>Decision Support System</li>
<li>Decision Making System</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Roles of MIS:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Plan effective resource allocation</li>
<li>Choose between alternative actions</li>
<li>Control daily operations</li>
</ul>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image14.webp$c$, 1, 'information-systems'),
  ($c$akis-for-farmers-western-rajasthan$c$, $c$Agriculture Knowledge Information System (AKIS) for Increasing Knowledge of Farmers in Western Rajasthan$c$, $c$D. Kumar, R. S. Mehta, Shiran K., S. C. Meena$c$, $c$AKIS is a network of organizations, individuals, and the interactions between them involved in the anticipation, creation, transformation, storage, retrieval, integration, diffusion, and utilization of agricultural knowledge and information. It systematically supports decision-making, problem-solving, and innovation in agriculture.$c$, $c$<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image16.webp" alt="Agriculture Knowledge Information System (AKIS) for Increasing Knowledge of Farmers in Western Rajasthan" loading="lazy"></figure>
<p>AKIS is a network of organizations, individuals, and the interactions between them involved in the anticipation, creation, transformation, storage, retrieval, integration, diffusion, and utilization of agricultural knowledge and information. It systematically supports decision-making, problem-solving, and innovation in agriculture.</p>
<p><strong>Key Elements:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Agricultural Research</li>
<li>Agricultural Education</li>
<li>Organizations</li>
<li>Rural Farming Community</li>
<li>Mass Media</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Models of AKIS:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>INTERPAKS Model (Swanson, 1985): Focuses on policy and knowledge transfer.</li>
<li>Elliot’s ATMS Model (1987): Agricultural Technology Management System model emphasizing technology application.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Functions / Importance (Nigel, 1980):</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Identify problems and knowledge needs at the procedural level.</li>
<li>Generate innovations.</li>
<li>Validate innovations under farmer conditions.</li>
<li>Operationalize innovations for practical use.</li>
<li>Disseminate knowledge.</li>
<li>Promote utilization.</li>
<li>Evaluate outcomes and experiences.</li>
</ol>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image16.webp$c$, 1, 'extension-akis'),
  ($c$contract-farming-jeera-isabgol-pomegranate-western-rajasthan$c$, $c$Contract Farming for Remunerative Prices of Jeera, Isabgol and Pomegranate in Western Rajasthan$c$, $c$D. Kumar, R. S. Mehta, Shiran K., S. C. Meena$c$, $c$Contract farming is an agreement between farmers and processing or marketing firms for the production and supply of agricultural products under predetermined conditions, often including price, quality, and technical support. Farmers commit to providing a specific quantity and quality of produce, while companies support production and purchase the output. This system helps distribute risks between…$c$, $c$<p>Contract farming is an agreement between farmers and processing or marketing firms for the production and supply of agricultural products under predetermined conditions, often including price, quality, and technical support. Farmers commit to providing a specific quantity and quality of produce, while companies support production and purchase the output. This system helps distribute risks between producers and buyers.</p>
<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image17.webp" alt="Contract Farming for Remunerative Prices of Jeera, Isabgol and Pomegranate in Western Rajasthan" loading="lazy"></figure>
<p><strong>Types of Contract Farming:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Market–Specification Contracts: Pre-harvest agreements specifying price, quality, and timing.</li>
<li>Resource-Providing Contracts: Companies supply inputs like seeds, fertilizers, machinery, or credit.</li>
<li>Production Management Contracts: Companies guide production methods and inputs, often combined with marketing agreements.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Benefits for Small Farmers:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Access to quality inputs, technical guidance, and management skills.</li>
<li>Improved overall farm management and yield.</li>
<li>Reduced marketing and price risks.</li>
<li>Legal protection under the Model APMC Act for contract registration, land rights, and dispute resolution.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Success Stories in India:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>PepsiCo (potato, tomato, groundnut, chili) in Punjab</li>
<li>Safflower in Madhya Pradesh</li>
<li>Oil palm in Andhra Pradesh</li>
<li>Amul and NDDB (milk procurement)</li>
<li>Sugarcane cooperatives in Maharashtra</li>
<li>Prawn aquaculture in Andhra Pradesh</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Challenges and Risks:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Breach of contracts by both parties due to market fluctuations.</li>
<li>Preference for medium and large farmers due to transaction costs; small farmers may need collective agreements.</li>
<li>Production risks are not always covered; crop failure may not be compensated.</li>
<li>Contract terms often lack transparency, limiting farmers’ bargaining power.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Evolution and Legal Context in India:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Existed since the 1960s for seed production.</li>
<li>Became widespread in the 1980s–1990s with perishable crops.</li>
<li>Legal framework strengthened by amendments to the APMC Act, allowing formal recognition of contract farming.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Corporate Farming:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Legally restricted in India; non-farmers cannot own land due to the Land Ceiling Act.</li>
<li>Attempts to lease land for corporate farming have faced challenges and local opposition.</li>
</ul>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image17.webp$c$, 2, 'contract-farming'),
  ($c$ict-for-agriculture-thar-desert$c$, $c$Importance of ICT for Agriculture Development in the Thar Desert of Rajasthan$c$, $c$D. Kumar, R. S. Mehta, Shiren K., S. C. Meena$c$, $c$The Thar Desert of Rajasthan is characterized by low and irregular rainfall, high temperatures, sandy soils, water scarcity, and frequent droughts. Agriculture in this region faces many challenges, including limited irrigation facilities, uncertain weather conditions, pest attacks, and inadequate access to markets and agricultural information. Information and Communication Technology (ICT) can…$c$, $c$<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image18.webp" alt="Importance of ICT for Agriculture Development in the Thar Desert of Rajasthan" loading="lazy"></figure>
<p>The Thar Desert of Rajasthan is characterized by low and irregular rainfall, high temperatures, sandy soils, water scarcity, and frequent droughts. Agriculture in this region faces many challenges, including limited irrigation facilities, uncertain weather conditions, pest attacks, and inadequate access to markets and agricultural information. Information and Communication Technology (ICT) can play an important role in addressing these challenges and improving agricultural productivity and farmers’ livelihoods.</p>
<h3>Importance of ICT in Agriculture</h3>
<h3>1. Weather Information</h3>
<p>ICT provides farmers with timely information about rainfall, temperature, droughts, storms, and other weather conditions. Mobile phones, weather applications, SMS services, and online platforms can help farmers decide when to sow, irrigate, harvest, and protect crops.</p>
<h3>2. Better Water Management</h3>
<p>Water is one of the most important resources in the Thar Desert. ICT-based systems can help farmers monitor soil moisture, rainfall, groundwater, and irrigation requirements. Smart irrigation technologies can reduce water wastage and improve the efficient use of scarce water resources.</p>
<h3>3. Access to Agricultural Knowledge</h3>
<p>Through mobile phones, websites, agricultural apps, and digital advisory services, farmers can obtain information about improved seeds, fertilizers, crop varieties, pest and disease management, organic farming, and modern agricultural practices.</p>
<h3>4. Pest and Disease Management</h3>
<p>ICT can provide early warnings and expert advice about crop pests and diseases. Farmers can use photographs and mobile-based advisory services to identify problems and receive recommendations, potentially reducing crop losses and unnecessary pesticide use.</p>
<h3>5. Market Information</h3>
<p>Digital platforms can provide information about crop prices in different markets. This helps farmers understand market conditions, identify potential buyers, and make more informed decisions about when and where to sell their produce.</p>
<h3>6. Access to Government Schemes</h3>
<p>ICT makes it easier for farmers to obtain information about government subsidies, crop insurance, agricultural loans, welfare programmes, and other schemes. Online applications and digital services can reduce the need for repeated visits to government offices.</p>
<h3>7. Promotion of Climate-Smart Agriculture</h3>
<p>The Thar Desert is highly vulnerable to climate variability. ICT can support climate-smart farming by providing information on drought-resistant crops, efficient irrigation, soil management, weather-based advisories, and sustainable farming practices.</p>
<h3>8. Improvement in Livestock Management</h3>
<p>Livestock is an important part of the rural economy of western Rajasthan. ICT can help farmers access information about animal health, vaccination, feeding, breeding, disease prevention, and veterinary services.</p>
<h3>9. Financial and Digital Services</h3>
<p>Mobile banking and digital payment systems can improve farmers’ access to financial services. ICT can also facilitate access to agricultural credit, insurance, digital payments, and other financial facilities.</p>
<h3>10. Improved Communication and Rural Development</h3>
<p>ICT connects farmers with agricultural universities, experts, government departments, markets, traders, and other farmers. Better communication can strengthen knowledge sharing and encourage the adoption of appropriate agricultural technologies.</p>
<h3>Challenges in Using ICT</h3>
<p>Despite its benefits, the use of ICT in the Thar Desert faces several challenges. These include poor internet connectivity in some rural areas, limited digital literacy, lack of awareness, electricity shortages, language barriers, and the cost of digital devices and services. Therefore, ICT programmes should be designed according to local needs and should provide information in simple and locally understandable languages.</p>
<p><strong>Key ICT Tools in Agriculture:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>GIS (Geographical Information System): Databases with geographic data, software tools for analysis and visualization, human expertise, and organizational support.</li>
<li>Remote Sensing: Acquisition of information about objects or phenomena without physical contact, used in agriculture, hydrology, ecology, meteorology, and land management.</li>
<li>E-Governance: Application of IT for delivering government services and facilitating communication between government, citizens, and businesses (G2C, G2B, G2G, G2E).</li>
<li>Cyber Extension: Managed virtual learning environments for agricultural education, training, and knowledge dissemination.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Role of ICT in Agricultural Development:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Support economic development, planning, and market information.</li>
<li>Promote community development and enterprise growth.</li>
<li>Facilitate research and education applications.</li>
<li>Enhance public administration, service delivery, and transparency.</li>
<li>Empower citizens with access to information and knowledge.</li>
<li>Improve training, employment opportunities, and decision-making.</li>
<li>Enable market linkages and provide services to small and medium farmers.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Conclusion</h3>
<p>ICT has considerable potential to support agricultural development in the Thar Desert of Rajasthan. By improving access to weather information, water-management technologies, agricultural knowledge, markets, government schemes, and financial services, ICT can help farmers make better decisions and use resources more efficiently. However, successful digital agriculture requires reliable connectivity, affordable technology, digital literacy, and strong support from agricultural institutions and government agencies.</p>$c$,
   $c$/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image18.webp$c$, 4, 'ict-in-agriculture'),
  ($c$pmddky-importance-in-jaisalmer$c$, $c$PMDDKY: Pradhan Mantri Dhan-Dhaanya Krishi Yojana – Importance in Jaisalmer$c$, $c$D. Kumar, Saren K., R. S. Mehta, S. C. Meena$c$, $c$The Pradhan Mantri Dhan-Dhaanya Krishi Yojana (PMDDKY) is a new government program started to make Indian agriculture more productive, modern, and profitable for farmers.It was announced in the Union Budget 2025–26 by Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman and approved by the Union Cabinet on 16 July 2025. The scheme will run for six years (2025–26 to 2030–31) with a total budget of ₹1.44 lakh crore…$c$, $c$<figure><img src="/articles/vol-1-issue-1/image19.webp" alt="PMDDKY: Pradhan Mantri Dhan-Dhaanya Krishi Yojana – Importance in Jaisalmer" loading="lazy"></figure>
<p>The Pradhan Mantri Dhan-Dhaanya Krishi Yojana (PMDDKY) is a new government program started to make Indian agriculture more productive, modern, and profitable for farmers.It was announced in the Union Budget 2025–26 by Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman and approved by the Union Cabinet on 16 July 2025. The scheme will run for six years (2025–26 to 2030–31) with a total budget of ₹1.44 lakh crore (₹24,000 crore each year).PMDDKY will focus on 100 backward districts where farming faces major problems like low yields, water shortage, and lack of modern tools. Around 1.7 crore small and marginal farmers (with land less than 2 hectares) will benefit from this scheme.</p>
<h3>Key Idea of PMDDKY</h3>
<p>PMDDKY brings together 36 existing agricultural schemes from 11 ministries—such as PM-KISAN (cash transfer), PMFBY (crop insurance), PMKSY (irrigation), and RKVY (development projects)—into one single program.</p>
<p>This helps reduce confusion, improve coordination, and make sure farmers get all benefits from one window.</p>
<p>It is inspired by the Aspirational Districts Programme of NITI Aayog, which improved health, education, and infrastructure in 112 poor districts. Similarly, PMDDKY aims to raise agricultural productivity, income, and sustainability in lagging districts.</p>
<h3>Why PMDDKY Was Started</h3>
<p><strong>Indian agriculture faces many problems that limit its growth:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Low Crop Productivity:In many districts (like Bundelkhand, Seemanchal, and tribal areas), crop yields are much lower than the national average due to poor soil, low irrigation, and old techniques.Example: Rice yield in Bihar’s Seemanchal area is only 1.8 tonnes/hectare compared to 2.7 tonnes/hectare nationally.</li>
<li>Dependence on Monsoons:Over 52% of farmland depends on rainfall. Droughts or floods cause major crop losses, like Bundelkhand’s 2023 drought that destroyed 30% of crops.</li>
<li>Small Landholdings:About 86% of farmers own less than 2 hectares of land and earn only around ₹10,000/month, which is not enough for family needs.</li>
<li>Lack of Modern Resources:Many farmers cannot afford quality seeds, bio-fertilizers, or modern machines.</li>
<li>Post-Harvest Losses:Around 20% of crops (like fruits and vegetables) spoil due to lack of storage, leading to losses worth ₹50,000 crore every year.</li>
<li>Low Income and Market Problems:Middlemen and weak markets reduce farmers’ earnings, causing debt and poverty.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Main Objectives of PMDDKY</h3>
<p><strong>PMDDKY aims to:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Increase crop yields by 20–30% using modern inputs.</li>
<li>Provide irrigation to reduce dependence on monsoons.</li>
<li>Support farm mechanization with affordable tools.</li>
<li>Develop storage and cold chains to reduce post-harvest losses to under 5%.</li>
<li>Improve market access and credit availability to double farmer income by 2030.</li>
<li>Promote organic and sustainable farming.</li>
<li>Empower women and youth in agriculture and allied sectors like dairy and fisheries.</li>
<li>Achieve self-reliance in foodgrains, pulses, and oilseeds.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Implementation and Monitoring</h3>
<p>The scheme will be managed by the Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers’ Welfare, with three levels of oversight:</p>
<ol>
<li>National Steering Committee – overall supervision.</li>
<li>State-Level Nodal Committees – coordination and execution.</li>
<li>District Dhan Dhaanya Samitis – headed by the District Collector, for local implementation.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Key Benefits of PMDDKY</h3>
<p>PMDDKY offers a comprehensive set of benefits designed to transform the agricultural landscape:</p>
<ol>
<li>Increased Crop Yields: Access to high-yielding seeds (e.g., hybrid wheat yielding 4 tonnes/hectare), bio-fertilizers, and mechanized tools like seed drills to boost production.</li>
<li>Higher Income: Diversifying into high-value crops like pulses (₹80-100/kg) and vegetables, and direct market access through apps to increase profits by 20-40%.</li>
<li>Sustainable Farming: Organic fertilizers, water-saving irrigation, and climate-resilient crops to maintain soil health and reduce environmental impact.</li>
<li>Irrigation Systems: Drip and sprinkler systems to ensure water availability, enabling year-round farming in dry regions.</li>
<li>Storage Facilities: Village and block-level warehouses and cold storage to prevent spoilage of 20% of perishables like fruits, vegetables, and dairy.</li>
<li>Financial Support: Subsidies (50-80% off inputs) and loans (short-term: ₹50,000–₹1 lakh; long-term: ₹1–10 lakh) through Kisan Credit Cards or NABARD.</li>
<li>Market Access: Digital platforms like e-NAM and new PMDDKY apps to connect farmers directly to buyers, reducing middlemen and boosting profits.</li>
<li>Training and Skill Development: Free workshops by Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs), agricultural universities, and private partners on modern farming, drone use, and allied activities like beekeeping.</li>
<li>Women Empowerment: Support for 10,000 women producer groups with training, loans, and market linkages for activities like dairy or organic farming.</li>
<li>Global Exposure: Fully funded international training for 500 farmers in countries like Israel (expertise in drip irrigation), Japan (precision farming), or the Netherlands (greenhouse technology).</li>
</ol>$c$,
